AVM backport: osxaudio: Drop in io_proc on pause instead of removing it

Upstream GStreamer commit efb5f4abc572be3fd0566261ff6e43aceb06c57e
(Nirbheek Chauhan, 2025-11-17, merge request 10107), which fixes
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/gstreamer/gstreamer/-/issues/4155,
backported to gst-plugins-good 1.19.1 as UTM patches it.

The deadlock it fixes is the one AVM sampled on 2026-07-12 (VMView.swift):
pausing set io_proc_needs_deactivation, and CoreAudio's IO thread then
removed the render callback from inside its own render notification,
waiting on the AudioUnit's mutex, while the SPICE worker sat in
AudioOutputUnitStop holding that mutex and waiting on the IO thread.
After this patch nothing removes the callback from the IO thread: pause
sets io_proc_dropping, and the sink writes silence (the source drops
samples) until the unit is started again or stopped.

Differences from upstream, both because 1.19.1 is older:
  - The sink hunk's rbuf refactor and the CORE_AUDIO_TIMING_LOCK context
    are left out; only the silence branch is taken.
  - The comment change in gst_core_audio_io_proc_stop is left out, since
    UTM's patch already moved that code.

Applied after UTM's own patches/gst-plugins-good-1.19.1.patch by
Tools/build-sysroot.sh.

--- a/sys/osxaudio/gstosxaudiosink.c
+++ b/sys/osxaudio/gstosxaudiosink.c
@@ -525,10 +525,18 @@
   gint stream_idx = buf->core_audio->stream_idx;
   gint remaining = bufferList->mBuffers[stream_idx].mDataByteSize;
   gint offset = 0;
+  GstAudioRingBuffer *rbuf = GST_AUDIO_RING_BUFFER (buf);
+  const GstAudioFormatInfo *finfo = rbuf->spec.info.finfo;
 
   while (remaining) {
-    if (!gst_audio_ring_buffer_prepare_read (GST_AUDIO_RING_BUFFER (buf),
-            &readseg, &readptr, &len))
+    if (g_atomic_int_get (&buf->core_audio->io_proc_dropping)) {
+      gst_audio_format_info_fill_silence (finfo, (char *)
+          bufferList->mBuffers[stream_idx].mData + offset, remaining);
+      GST_TRACE_OBJECT (buf, "Wrote silence, %d samples", remaining);
+      return 0;
+    }
+
+    if (!gst_audio_ring_buffer_prepare_read (rbuf, &readseg, &readptr, &len))
       return 0;
 
     len -= buf->segoffset;
